      "a": "Summary\nSummary: list my links. WHERE user_id. This is a hands-on lab, not a riddle. You should be able to explain the function out loud, then type it from memory. What the code is doing: Read the snippet under this question. The green comments on the right say what each line is doing. A line you can point at: SELECT id, url FROM links WHERE user_id = $1; In an interview, start with the job in plain words (the first sentences here), then walk the snippet. Do not start with a shop story. When you are done: run it, break it with empty input or a wrong id, then fix the check. That is the whole point of a practice row. The page matches the list. After Create/Update/Delete you Read again (or return the new array). Interviewers want GET as the safe method that does not change data. GET must not delete or create. Do not send another user's rows. If the list is huge, paginate. replaceChildren before append or you duplicate cards.\n\nThe problem before\nThe data existed in memory or SQL, but the screen showed a hardcoded list. After add or delete, the page lied. Two tabs showed different truth because nobody read from one source. This lab exists because that failure is what interviews and real apps hit. WHERE user_id. GET must not delete or create. Do not send another user's rows. If the list is huge, paginate. replaceChildren before append or you duplicate cards.\n\nWhat this is\nWHERE user_id. Read means you take the current list and show it. GET /todos returns JSON. showAll wipes the old nodes and paints each object. SELECT returns rows. You do not invent items in the render function. Read the snippet under this question. The green comments on the right say what each line is doing. The page matches the list. After Create/Update/Delete you Read again (or return the new array). Interviewers want GET as the safe method that does not change data.\n\nWhat it solves\nThe page matches the list. After Create/Update/Delete you Read again (or return the new array). Interviewers want GET as the safe method that does not change data. After this row you can demo the happy path and one failed path (empty input, wrong password, or missing id). WHERE user_id. Say the job in one sentence, then point at the code, then name one mistake.\n\nReal-life example\nA notice board. The clerk does not remember posters from yesterday. They look at the drawer (the array or table) and pin every paper that is still there. If the drawer is empty, the board is empty. Map guest → user, counter → this function, back room → the array, file, or database in the snippet.\n\nUses\nHome lists, admin tables, GET /me, SELECT ... FROM todos WHERE user_id = $1. React mapping an array to cards. On this sheet, the question is: Practice: list my links. Use the same idea in the next lab instead of copying a new pattern. The page matches the list. After Create/Update/Delete you Read again (or return the new array). Interviewers want GET as the safe method that does not change data.\n\nWatch out\nGET must not delete or create. Do not send another user's rows. If the list is huge, paginate. replaceChildren before append or you duplicate cards. Read the comments on the right before you paste. If a line looks clever and has no comment, it is the line that will fail in an interview. WHERE user_id.",
